% ============================================================================
% opposites — a common word and EVERY opposite (antonym) its Wiktionary
% Antonyms line names (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% One of the very first things a child learns about words: many of them come in
% pairs that mean the reverse of each other. HOT is the opposite of COLD, BIG is
% the opposite of SMALL. The word for "the opposite of" is an ANTONYM. This
% library records clean word→opposite pairs so an ADJ program can recall them,
% each one cited to a dictionary. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? opposite(hot, $Word)      % cold ; chilled
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `opposite(word, opposite)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns EVERY
% opposite WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and ABSTAINS on
% any word not in the table (it never invents an opposite it cannot cite). Some
% words are deliberately ONE-TO-MANY: the source's own Antonyms line names
% several opposites for `hot`/`big`/`happy`/`open`, not just one, so
% `opposite(happy, $Word)` binds `$Word` to EACH of them in turn — the same
% single-valued-to-many-valued shape `synonyms.adj` already established for
% its sibling table.
%
% The pairs, as a truth table:
%
%     word    opposite     read as…
%     ------  -----------  ----------------------
%     hot     cold         "the opposite of hot is cold"
%     hot     chilled
%     big     small        "the opposite of big is small"
%     big     little
%     big     tiny
%     big     minuscule
%     big     miniature
%     big     minute
%     fast    slow         "the opposite of fast is slow"
%     wet     dry          "the opposite of wet is dry"
%     happy   sad          "the opposite of happy is sad"
%     happy   blue
%     happy   depressed
%     happy   down
%     happy   miserable
%     happy   moody
%     happy   morose
%     happy   unhappy
%     open    closed       "the opposite of open is closed"
%     open    shut
%     hard    soft         "the opposite of hard is soft"
%
% Only ONE direction is shipped per pair (word → its opposite). A query like
% `? opposite(cold, $W)` therefore ABSTAINS: the honest-abstention property is
% deliberate — the table answers only what its source explicitly lists, and a
% word with no shipped opposite (e.g. `purple`) returns no invented answer.
%
% Provenance — nothing here is from memory. Each pair is copied from the English
% Wiktionary entry for the FIRST word, whose "Antonyms" line lists the
% opposites verbatim. The per-word citable spans (quoted exactly as printed)
% are:
%
%     hot    → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hot     "Antonyms: cold, chilled"
%     big    → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/big     "little, small, tiny, minuscule, miniature, minute"
%     fast   → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fast    "Antonyms: slow"  (sense: quick/rapid)
%     wet    → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wet     "Antonym: dry"
%     happy  → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/happy   "blue, depressed, down, miserable, moody, morose, sad, unhappy"
%     open   → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/open    "closed, shut"  (sense: accessible)
%     hard   → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hard    "Antonym: soft"  (sense: resistant to pressure)
%
% EXTENDED this cycle from 7 to 21 rows: the header ABOVE already quoted the
% full Antonyms line for `hot`/`big`/`happy`/`open` when this table originally
% shipped — only the FIRST antonym in each of those four lines had ever been
% turned into a row, even though the same already-quoted span names one more
% (hot), five more (big), seven more (happy), and one more (open). `fast`,
% `wet`, and `hard` were already fully captured (their sources name only one
% antonym each). Zero new source page needed; WebFetch re-verified all seven
% Wiktionary "Antonyms" lines live before writing (confirming each word's
% already-quoted span is accurate, unchanged, and, for `big`, that the header's
% own truncating "…" in fact stopped at exactly six total antonyms). This
% mirrors `synonyms.adj`'s single-to-many-valued-per-key extension shape,
% needing no new WebFetch to DISCOVER the extra values for four of the seven
% words — they were already sitting in this table's own header the whole time.
%
% The single `source` span below is the clearest one (the `hot` entry); the
% `locator` points at that entry, and each remaining word's own page + verbatim
% span is quoted above so every row is independently checkable. `trust
% consensus` — a dictionary is a secondary reference, not a primary authority.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table opposite {
    columns word, opposite

    row (hot, cold)
    row (hot, chilled)
    row (big, small)
    row (big, little)
    row (big, tiny)
    row (big, minuscule)
    row (big, miniature)
    row (big, minute)
    row (fast, slow)
    row (wet, dry)
    row (happy, sad)
    row (happy, blue)
    row (happy, depressed)
    row (happy, down)
    row (happy, miserable)
    row (happy, moody)
    row (happy, morose)
    row (happy, unhappy)
    row (open, closed)
    row (open, shut)
    row (hard, soft)

    source "Antonyms: cold, chilled"
    locator "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hot"
    trust consensus
}
